    "content": "drift. They build repeatable preprocessing pipelines so training and inference apply the same transformations. Embeddings map text, images, or other objects into vectors where semantic similarity can be measured. Retrieval-augmented generation uses embeddings and a vector store to retrieve relevant context before asking a language model to produce an answer. Retrieval quality depends on chunking, metadata, query construction, embedding model, and reranking. RAG systems should be evaluated beyond subjective answer quality. Useful metrics include retrieval precision, recall at k, groundedness, answer faithfulness, latency, token cost, and user task success. Failure modes include irrelevant retrieval, missing context, hallucination, stale knowledge, and prompt injection. Model evaluation should match real-world usage. Accuracy can be misleading for imbalanced datasets, so precision, recall, F1 score, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, calibration, and confusion matrices are important. For ranking or retrieval tasks, mean reciprocal rank and normalized discounted cumulative",
