  {
    "id": "7420b61c7ff8cf7a8037ecdd950d7884cbde1a1ebf20647c2728d8519cc596f6",
    "role": "backend_engineer",
    "source": "backend_engineer.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "content": "request handling, business services, persistence, and background jobs. Services should validate inputs at the edge, keep domain rules in a service layer, and isolate database access through repositories or explicit query modules. This separation makes the codebase testable and easier to evolve. Authentication verifies identity, while authorization decides what that identity can do. JWTs are common for stateless APIs, but they need short lifetimes, secure signing, refresh-token handling, and careful revocation strategy. Role-based access control is simple to reason about, while attribute-based rules handle more complex policy decisions. Distributed systems introduce partial failure, retries, duplicate messages, race conditions, and eventual consistency. Reliable systems use idempotency keys, optimistic locking, durable queues, circuit breakers, timeouts, backoff, and structured logging. Candidates should be able to reason about consistency, availability, and operational tradeoffs. Caching improves latency and reduces load, but cache invalidation is difficult.",
