    "source": "backend_engineer.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "content": "circuit breakers, timeouts, backoff, and structured logging. Candidates should be able to reason about consistency, availability, and operational tradeoffs. Caching improves latency and reduces load, but cache invalidation is difficult. Common patterns include read-through caching, write-through caching, TTL-based freshness, and explicit invalidation after writes. A strong answer discusses stale data, cache stampede prevention, key design, and metrics. Observability includes logs, metrics, traces, and health checks. Production services should expose request latency, error rates, throughput, dependency failures, database timings, and queue depth. Good interview answers connect observability to debugging real incidents and measuring service-level objectives. Testing backend systems requires unit tests for business rules, integration tests for API and database behavior, and contract tests when services communicate. Good tests isolate external systems, seed realistic data, and verify error paths as well as happy paths.",
